"""Detects the "qi" word-meaning written in CJK or Latin letters.

What it responds to: the syllable/word "qi" (breath, air, vital energy, spirit), as a
single token. The strongest responses are the characters 气 (Simplified) / 氣 (Traditional)
/ 気 (Japanese) meaning "qi, air, spirit", and the Latin spelling "Qi" (also "khí" in
Vietnamese). A Korean 기 (the same "gi" sound, as in 하기) responds about half as strongly.
Weaker responses come from words that contain 气 or 気 (人气, 天气, 运气), from 汽
(steam) and from 器 (vessel), which share the sound but not the "qi" meaning.
Every other token is predicted as 0.

Position in a document does not matter, so the program carries no state.
"""

READS = ()

# Typical activation for each kind of token (fit on sample text).
QI_CHARS = {"气": 7.2, "氣": 7.2, "気": 7.2}
ROMAN_QI = {"qi": 5.6, "khí": 5.6}
OTHER = {"汽": 4.9, "器": 1.6, "기": 4.0}
COMPOUND_QI = 1.9


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    tok = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    if tok.strip() in QI_CHARS:
        return carry, QI_CHARS[tok.strip()]
    if tok.strip().lower() in ROMAN_QI:
        return carry, ROMAN_QI[tok.strip().lower()]
    if tok.strip() in OTHER:
        return carry, OTHER[tok.strip()]
    if "기" in tok:
        return carry, 4.0
    if "Qi" in tok:
        return carry, 5.6
    if any(ch in tok for ch in "气気氣"):
        return carry, COMPOUND_QI
    return carry, 0.0

